  "slug": "avoidance-conflict-integration-resolves-848c",
  "summary": "New rodent work maps how insula, prethalamus and prelimbic cortex resolve threat-reward conflict, alongside dopamine and cerebellar roles in adaptive behavior.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is how rodent circuits arbitrate behavior under threat. In an approach-avoidance task, rats occupied three latent behavioral states, and anterior insular activity tracked the active state rather than integrated outcomes, arguing that conflict is resolved by state selection. A preprint identifies inhibitory prethalamic vLGN as promoting risk-taking under threat, with opposing retrosplenial and ventromedial hypothalamic inputs shaping exploratory 'courage.' Prelimbic single units encode diverse danger-modulated behaviors and the learned probability structure of threat during probabilistic fear conditioning. A second thread concerns dopamine and flexible control. Nigral dopamine shapes dorsomedial striatal ramping, including down-ramping MSNs, during interval timing. Ventral striatal dopamine rises after hippocampal sharp-wave ripples, more strongly during offline rest and with longer, higher-power ripples. Mid-posterior cerebellar Purkinje cells encode reinforcement-learning parameters, and lobule VIa photostimulation speeds contingency adaptation. Together, the work favors state- and probability-based representations over simple outcome summation.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-28"
}
